#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125612} :activity/id #uuid "23c64a39-d7c6-5e7e-a49e-62d8fec54931" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707 :topic/id #uuid "b6ff39e3-80ec-5a87-8943-dd2c886031a8" :topic/math-academy-id 1929} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125866 :tutorial/content "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function of two variables. The **partial derivative of** $\\boldsymbol{f}$ **with respect to** $\\boldsymbol x$ is computed by taking the derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ using the usual methods, *while treating $y$ as a constant.*\n\nThe partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ has the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial x}\n$$\n\nThis partial derivative is defined as the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's worth taking a moment to compare this to the derivative of a single-variable function $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also define the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y,$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial y}\t\t = \\lim_{h \\to 0}\\left(\\dfrac {f (x ,y + h) - f(x, y)} {h}\\right)\n$$\n\nIn this case, we'd differentiate $f$ with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant.\n\nWe also have the following notations for partial derivatives:\n\n$$\nf_x =  \\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial x}, \\qquad  f_y = \\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial y}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "aa94ef52-583b-5724-8870-449d64d7f611" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6922 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "bc70b9fc-e127-52de-b629-7d73fc229d30" :step/math-academy-id 41895 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125617}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124639 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124197 :question/id #uuid "35a664b8-a895-5c52-a92f-b62c75b44744" :question/math-academy-id "e-11026"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53c5d5d7-40d4-5210-8794-428cffc1f3f9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381237}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Partial Derivatives of Two-Variable Functions"} :step/id #uuid "cc3cd58f-20da-51fd-9b2b-5978711ef072" :step/math-academy-id 41896 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124643 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124201 :question/id #uuid "a14d53ae-1922-531d-bb2a-bda9b4829f6e" :question/math-academy-id "e-11045"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59c3f441-0499-575c-af10-b597d61e6e35" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048904}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381188}] :knowledge-point/title "Partial Derivatives as Limits of Difference Quotients"} :step/id #uuid "214ecda8-f25e-5a40-aeb0-50074bc2c6e3" :step/math-academy-id 41966 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125867 :tutorial/content "Consider the multivariable function $f(x,y),$ given by\n\n$$\nf (x, y)= x y^2 + e^{y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to ${\\color{blue}x},$ we \"fix\" the variable $y$ (in other words, treat $y$ as if it were a constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to ${\\color{blue}x},$ as we usually would:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+{e}^{y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot (1)+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the term $e^{y}$ vanished when taking the derivative with respect to $x.$ This is because $e^y$ is a function of $y$ only, and since we're treating $y$ as a constant, this entire term is treated as a constant and therefore differentiates to zero (just as a constant would).\n\nSimilarly, to compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to ${\\color{blue}y},$ we fix the variable $x$ and then take the derivative with respect to ${\\color{blue}y},$ as we usually would:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+{e}^{y}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (2y)+({e}^{y}) \\\\  & =2xy+{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen we wish to evaluate a partial derivative at a point $(x_0, y_0),$ we can use any of the following notations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f(x_0,y_0)} {\\partial x} =  \\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial x}\\Bigg|_{(x_0,y_0)} = f_x(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nThe notations for $f_y(x_0,y_0)$ follow the same patterns." :tutorial/id #uuid "970fa195-eccd-5861-b9c3-9190d032b128" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6930 :tutorial/title "Computing Partial Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "91336c4e-e6ff-580a-befb-b1eda33e20ae" :step/math-academy-id 41969 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124644 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124202 :question/id #uuid "ee2294e1-1e9a-5be5-be47-4c2e295b640a" :question/math-academy-id "e-11049"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "facdd9e7-8fcb-5ad1-b9c1-82c0f753df1a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381209}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Partial Derivative at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "f53ec977-578e-53da-954c-e73865ef8901" :step/math-academy-id 41990}] :activity/title "Introduction to Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124722}]}
